KG42.register("rules-ea8b508c48daa8ddc7e5914e",{"id":"31-coalition_formation::v141-c-R003","paper":"31-coalition_formation","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R003","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Energy-trade execution","process_stage":"activation","market_meaning":"Splitting from the previous coalition and merging with state-2 peers precedes trading between receivers and providers within coalition 2. Scope: Reconfiguration into coalition 2 and subsequent trading within that coalition at time slot t. Condition: A prosumer in state 1 at t-1 chooses state 2 at t and changes coalitions. State-1 trading within coalition 1 uses the mid-market pricing scheme referenced in [39]. Scope: P2P trading among members of coalition 1. Condition: Trading prosumers are in state 1. Trading within coalition 2 uses the local trading prices derived in (18) and (19). Scope: P2P trading between receivers and providers within coalition 2. Condition: Trading prosumers are in state 2. The pricing design removes the possibility of a contemporaneous trade between prosumers who do not charge/discharge batteries for trading and prosumers who do. Scope: Same-time-slot trades between prosumers who use batteries for trading and prosumers who do not. Condition: Prices without battery-degradation compensation fall within case 3 of Table 2, as specified for the proposed model. State-1 trading assumes zero contribution of battery energy to flows across trading entities. Scope: Battery-energy flow across trading entities in state-1 trading. Condition: The prosumer is in state 1. State-2 trading permits selling energy discharged from a battery to other prosumers and buying peer energy to charge a battery for future needs. Scope: Battery-energy exchanges with other peers in the P2P network. Condition: The prosumer is in state 2. The source constrains battery exchange so charging and discharging exceed neither the rated battery rate nor the network energy-transfer limit; the network transfer cap protects recommended node-voltage limits. Scope: State-2 battery-energy exchanges through the P2P network. Condition: Battery charging or discharging is undertaken for P2P trading at time t. Total energy charged by receivers must equal total energy discharged by providers. Scope: Aggregate battery-energy exchange between providers and receivers in coalition V. Condition: State-2 providers and receivers exchange battery energy through P2P trading. The baseline model does not include state-2 sales of battery energy to the CPS. Scope: Baseline proposed model, excluding the discussed CPS-as-peer extension. Condition: A state-2 prosumer would sell battery energy to the CPS. In this proposed extension, a prosumer may sell energy to the CPS through the P2P mechanism. Scope: Conditional CPS-as-peer extension discussed in Remark 1. Condition: The CPS participates in the local market as a peer and allows prosumers to negotiate the per-unit price of energy sold to it.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function execution.energy_trade under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["execution.energy_trade","matching.counterparties","pricing.local"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R003-E001","source":"matching.counterparties","target":"execution.energy_trade","type":"precedes","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R003-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-M01-E24"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R003-E002","source":"pricing.local","target":"execution.energy_trade","type":"limits","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R003-C004"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E26"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R003-E003","source":"pricing.local","target":"execution.energy_trade","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R003-C002","v141-c-R003-C003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-M01-E24"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"activation","context_text":"Aggregate battery-energy exchange between providers and receivers in coalition V. | Baseline proposed model, excluding the discussed CPS-as-peer extension. | Battery-energy exchanges with other peers in the P2P network. | Battery-energy flow across trading entities in state-1 trading. | Conditional CPS-as-peer extension discussed in Remark 1. | P2P trading among members of coalition 1. | P2P trading between receivers and providers within coalition 2. | Reconfiguration into coalition 2 and subsequent trading within that coalition at time slot t. | Same-time-slot trades between prosumers who use batteries for trading and prosumers who do not. | State-2 battery-energy exchanges through the P2P network.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-M01-E24","v141-b-g01-E009","v141-b-g01-E010","v141-b-g01-E013","v141-b-g01-E014","v141-b-g02-E26","v141-b-g02-E31","v141-b-g02-E34"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"x_R003_enc","symbol":"e_{n,c}(t)","meaning":"Energy charged by prosumer n's battery at time slot t for state-2 P2P trading, bounded by the source's Eq. (7).","kind":"operational","domain":"Energy quantity for battery charging at time slot t, subject to the source Eq. (7) bounds; numerical unit is not stated in the supplied pages.","unit":null,"indices":["n","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E013","v141-b-g02-E04"]},{"variable_id":"x_R003_end","symbol":"e_{n,d}(t)","meaning":"Energy discharged from prosumer n's battery at time slot t for state-2 P2P trading, bounded by the source's Eq. (7).","kind":"operational","domain":"Energy quantity for battery discharging at time slot t, subject to the source Eq. (7) bounds; numerical unit is not stated in the supplied pages.","unit":null,"indices":["n","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E013","v141-b-g02-E04"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"theta_R003_Echrdis","symbol":"E_{chr,dis}","meaning":"Rated charging/discharging rate of the battery used in source Eq. (8).","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"source-defined rated charging/discharging rate; numeric value not supplied"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E011","v141-b-g01-E014"]},{"parameter_id":"theta_R003_Etransfer","symbol":"E_{transfer}(t)","meaning":"Network-controller-imposed maximum limit on energy transfer over the network at time t such that node voltage does not violate the recommended reliability and security limit.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"source-defined time-varying network energy-transfer limit; numeric values not supplied"},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E014"]},{"parameter_id":"theta_R003_emax","symbol":"e_{\\max}(t)","meaning":"Effective maximum battery charging/discharging energy rate in Eq. (7), printed in Eq. (8) as the maximum of E_{chr,dis} and E_{transfer}(t).","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"\\max(E_{chr,dis}, E_{transfer}(t))"},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E014","v141-b-g02-E04"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Splitting from the previous coalition and merging with state-2 peers precedes trading between receivers and providers within coalition 2. Scope: Reconfiguration into coalition 2 and subsequent trading within that coalition at time slot t. Condition: A prosumer in state 1 at t-1 chooses state 2 at t and changes coalitions.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties","execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-M01-E24"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"State-1 trading within coalition 1 uses the mid-market pricing scheme referenced in [39]. Scope: P2P trading among members of coalition 1. Condition: Trading prosumers are in state 1.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-M01-E24"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Trading within coalition 2 uses the local trading prices derived in (18) and (19). Scope: P2P trading between receivers and providers within coalition 2. Condition: Trading prosumers are in state 2.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-M01-E24"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C004","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"The pricing design removes the possibility of a contemporaneous trade between prosumers who do not charge/discharge batteries for trading and prosumers who do. Scope: Same-time-slot trades between prosumers who use batteries for trading and prosumers who do not. Condition: Prices without battery-degradation compensation fall within case 3 of Table 2, as specified for the proposed model.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E26"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"State-1 trading assumes zero contribution of battery energy to flows across trading entities. Scope: Battery-energy flow across trading entities in state-1 trading. Condition: The prosumer is in state 1.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E010"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C006","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"State-2 trading permits selling energy discharged from a battery to other prosumers and buying peer energy to charge a battery for future needs. Scope: Battery-energy exchanges with other peers in the P2P network. Condition: The prosumer is in state 2.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E009"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C007","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The source constrains battery exchange so charging and discharging exceed neither the rated battery rate nor the network energy-transfer limit; the network transfer cap protects recommended node-voltage limits. Scope: State-2 battery-energy exchanges through the P2P network. Condition: Battery charging or discharging is undertaken for P2P trading at time t.","expression":"e_{n,c}(t) \\le \\min(e_{\\max}(t), b_{n,c}(t), E_{n,sur}(t)), \\quad e_{n,d}(t) \\le \\min(e_{\\max}(t), s_n, E_{n,def}(t))","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":["x_R003_enc","x_R003_end"],"parameter_refs":["theta_R003_emax"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E013","v141-b-g01-E014"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C008","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Total energy charged by receivers must equal total energy discharged by providers. Scope: Aggregate battery-energy exchange between providers and receivers in coalition V. Condition: State-2 providers and receivers exchange battery energy through P2P trading.","expression":"E_d(t) = E_c(t)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E31"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C009","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The baseline model does not include state-2 sales of battery energy to the CPS. Scope: Baseline proposed model, excluding the discussed CPS-as-peer extension. Condition: A state-2 prosumer would sell battery energy to the CPS.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E34"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C010","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"In this proposed extension, a prosumer may sell energy to the CPS through the P2P mechanism. Scope: Conditional CPS-as-peer extension discussed in Remark 1. Condition: The CPS participates in the local market as a peer and allows prosumers to negotiate the per-unit price of energy sold to it.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E34"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C011","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"As printed in Eq. (8), the effective maximum battery energy rate e_{\\max}(t) is the maximum of the rated charging/discharging rate E_{chr,dis} and the network energy-transfer limit E_{transfer}(t); the surrounding source text says battery charging or discharging must exceed neither the rated rate nor the network transfer limit, so the printed max form is retained and flagged for review.","expression":"e_{\\max}(t) = \\max(E_{chr,dis}, E_{transfer}(t))","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["execution.energy_trade"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["theta_R003_emax","theta_R003_Echrdis","theta_R003_Etransfer"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E014","v141-b-g02-E04"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-M01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p07-c1r1__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"W. Tushar, et al. trading, which is determined by a mid-market rate rule [39], a pro- ha sumer decid","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Prosumers that are in state 1 form coalition 1 (Fig. 1) and conduct P2P trading among themselves using the mechanism proposed in [39] via a mid-market pricing scheme. As for prosumers in state 2, each prosumer needs to decide either be a provider or a receiver of energy and thus be in the set $\\mathcal{K}$ or $\\mathcal{L}$ respectively. Once the decision is taken by prosumers, as shown in Fig. 2, each prosumer $n$ who was in state 1 in the previous time slot $t-1$ and would like to be in state 2 at $t$, splits itself from its previous coalition and merges with the players who decide to be in state 2 and thus becomes a part of a new coalition. A similar transition can also happen for state 2 players. Finally, P2P trading between the receivers and providers within coalition 2 is conducted using prices derived in (18) and (19)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E009","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"2. System model","anchor_text":"a its battery in the energy trading market. Similarly, a state 1 prosumer in only buys energy, when ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":"Table 1","image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We further consider that the prosumers participating in the P2P energy trading choose to be either of the two states at any given time slot t.\n(a) State 1 Prosumers in state 1 do not charge or discharge their batteries for trading purposes and only sell their surplus E_{n,sur}(t) to other prosumers in N\\{n\\} or to the grid when E_{n,sur}(t) = (E_{n,hou}(t) + E_{n,cha}(t)) - E_{n,pv}(t) > 0. In other words, a prosumer in state 1, which we denote as an element of set W, does not put its battery in the energy trading market. Similarly, a state 1 prosumer only buys energy, when necessary, to meet the deficient energy E_{n,def}(t) = (E_{n,pv}(t) + E_{n,dis}(t)) - E_{n,hou}(t). Note that a prosumer without any battery always remains in state 1.\n(b) State 2 Prosumers is state 2 use their batteries for trading purposes. That is, a prosumer in state 2, which we indicate via V is motivated, e.g., for more economical benefit, to discharge its battery for selling energy to other prosumers. Similarly, it may also buy energy from other peers to charge its battery for future needs.\nNote that when a prosumer does not have a battery (prosumer 6) or it does not want to charge or discharge despite having battery (prosumer 5 in time slot 1) will remain in state 1."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E010","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.1 Utility of prosumer at state 1","anchor_text":"battery energy across different entities. Hence, from the perspective of to P2P trading, each prosum","equation_id":"4","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For prosumer at state 1 the contribution of a prosumer’s battery to the energy trading can be assumed to be 0 when considering the flow of battery energy across different entities. Hence, from the perspective of P2P trading, each prosumer n at state 1 can be considered as a prosumer with the grid-tie solar system without battery [43]. Therefore, prosumers of this type trade their solar energy either with one another prosumer or with the grid without charging or discharging their batteries. In this context, if the mid-market buying and selling prices per unit of energy are p_b(t) and p_s(t) respectively (following [39]), the utility and cost to a prosumer n can be defined as\nU_{n,s}(t) = p_s(t)E_{n,sur}(t), and J_{n,b}(t) = p_b(t)E_{n,def}(t) (4)\nrespectively for selling and buying their surplus and deficient energy."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E013","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.2 Utility of a prosumer at state 2","anchor_text":"states by prosumer 1 and 2 from timeslot 1 to 2 Prosumer 3, 4, 5 and 6 remain in the same state in b","equation_id":"6-7","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"e_{n,c}(t) \\le \\min(e_{\\max}(t), b_{n,c}(t), E_{n,sur}(t)), \\quad e_{n,d}(t) \\le \\min(e_{\\max}(t), s_n, E_{n,def}(t)) (7)\nto ensure that battery charges or discharges neither more than its rated charging and discharging rate nor more than the network limit on the energy transfer. In other words,"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E014","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.2 Utility of a prosumer at state 2","anchor_text":"of to ensure that battery charges or discharges neither more than its rated er charging and discharg","equation_id":"8","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"e_{\\max}(t) = \\max(E_{chr,dis}, E_{transfer}(t)), (8)\nwhere, E_{transfer}(t) is network controller imposed maximum limit of energy transfer over the network at t such that the node voltage does not violate the recommended limit for reliability and security of the network."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E26","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p07-c1r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.3 Coalition formation algorithm","anchor_text":"tion structure, there is no coalition of prosumers who do not charge/ discharge batteries with who w","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Here, it is important to note that, in the proposed coalition formation structure, there is no coalition of prosumers who do not charge/discharge batteries with who will discharge/charge. This is due to the fact that, as the proposed scheme is designed, the charging and discharging price needs to satisfy the conditions explained in Table 2, where the threshold price $p_{n,c}(t)$ and $p_{n,d}(t)$ include the associated charging and discharging degradation cost of batteries. The motivation behind the choice of such prices is the fact that when a prosumer may want its battery to be charged or discharged for trading in the peer-to-peer market, the trading price should reflect the associated battery degradation cost due to charging and discharging, and at the same time, have enough incentive to make the price beneficial for prosumers to put their batteries into the market. Now, prosumers who participate in peer-to-peer trading market without battery do not need to consider such degradation cost and therefore the trading price does not include the additional item to compensate for the cost of battery degradation [39,51]. Given this context, as the proposed model is designed, these prices will always fall within the case 3 of Table 2, which subsequently removes the possibility of a trade between prosumers who do not charge/discharge batteries with prosumers who will discharge/charge at the same time."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E31","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.4 Trading of energy","anchor_text":"prosumer needs to pay the government tax and a subscription fee, ca captured via , for using the tra","equation_id":"18-19","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Now, the flow of energy for charging and discharging batteries via P2P establishes that the total energy charged by the receivers batteries should be equal to the total energy discharged by the providers in $\\mathcal{V}$, i.e., $E_d(t)=E_c(t)$."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E34","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p08-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"Remark 1","anchor_text":"network. Remark 1. Please note that in the manuscript we do not consider the an scenario, in which a","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Remark 1. Please note that in the manuscript we do not consider the scenario, in which a prosumer at state 2 can sell its battery energy to the CPS. This mainly due to the fact that such a trading model relies on a pricing scheme set by the CPS that provides prosumers with very limited benefits [4]. Consequently, as articulated in [53], battery at residential premises is not economically viable. Nonetheless, if a CPS can participate in the local market as a peer and give prosumers to negotiate the price for per unit energy it would sell to the CPS, the proposed model can easily be extended by including CPS as a peer. In such cases, a prosumer can also sell its energy to the CPS through P2P mechanism."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E011","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.2 Utility of a prosumer at state 2","anchor_text":"thefact that the benefit to a prosumer for charging its battery even- wpr tually gets saturated with","equation_id":"5","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"We note that the utility that a prosumer obtains from charging its battery can be defined as the sum of the relevant cost and the benefit of charging. Benefit of charging can be captured by using a utility function with decreasing marginal benefit [34,44,45]. Such a property refers to the fact that the benefit to a prosumer for charging its battery eventually gets saturated with charging as the SoC s_n(t) of the battery becomes close to its available capacity b_{n,c}(t) = b_n - s_n(t).\nTo this end, we consider the utility of a state 2 prosumer n for charging its battery as:\nU_{n,c}(t) = k\\left[b_{n,c}(t)e_{n,c}(t) - \\frac{1}{2}\\alpha_n e_{n,c}(t)^2\\right] - (p_l + p_c(t))e_{n,c}(t). (5)\nIn (5), the term \\left(b_{n,c}(t)e_{n,c}(t) - \\frac{1}{2}\\alpha_n e_{n,c}(t)^2\\right) refers to the benefit of charging, 0 \u003c k \\le 1 is a scaling factor, and (p_l + p_c(t))e_{n,c}(t) is the cost of charging. Here, p_l is the degradation cost per kWh of the battery [46], p_c(t) is the price per unit of charging energy e_{n,c}(t) \\le E_{chr,dis} (where, E_{chr,dis} is the rated charging/discharging rate of the battery), and \\alpha_n > 0 is the satisfaction parameter of prosumer n [44]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-e3722a409d-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.2 Utility of a prosumer at state 2","anchor_text":"of to ensure that battery charges or discharges neither more than its rated er charging and discharg","equation_id":"8","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Further, $e_{n,c}(t)\\le \\min(e_{\\max}(t), b_{n,c}(t), E_{n,sur}(t))$, $e_{n,d}(t)\\le \\min(e_{\\max}(t), s_n, E_{n,def}(t))$ (7), to ensure that battery charges or discharges neither more than its rated charging and discharging rate nor more than the network limit on the energy transfer. In other words, $e_{\\max}=\\max(E_{chr,dis}, E_{transfer}(t))$ (8), where, $E_{transfer}(t)$ is network controller imposed maximum limit of energy transfer over the network at $t$ such that the node voltage does not violate the recommended limit for reliability and security of the network."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-participation","v141-a-M01-pricing"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"execution.energy_trade","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-M01-E24","quote":"splits itself from its previous coalition and merges with the players who decide to be in state 2 and thus becomes a part of a new coalition. A similar transition can also happen for state 2 players. Finally, P2P trading between the receivers and providers within coalition 2 is conducted using prices derived in (18) and (19)."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-M01-E24","quote":"Prosumers that are in state 1 form coalition 1 (Fig. 1) and conduct P2P trading among themselves using the mechanism proposed in [39] via a mid-market pricing scheme."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-M01-E24","quote":"Finally, P2P trading between the receivers and providers within coalition 2 is conducted using prices derived in (18) and (19)."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E26","quote":"the trading price does not include the additional item to compensate for the cost of battery degradation [39,51]. Given this context, as the proposed model is designed, these prices will always fall within the case 3 of Table 2, which subsequently removes the possibility of a trade between prosumers who do not charge/discharge batteries with prosumers who will discharge/charge at the same time."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E010","quote":"For prosumer at state 1 the contribution of a prosumer’s battery to the energy trading can be assumed to be 0 when considering the flow of battery energy across different entities."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E009","quote":"That is, a prosumer in state 2, which we indicate via V is motivated, e.g., for more economical benefit, to discharge its battery for selling energy to other prosumers. Similarly, it may also buy energy from other peers to charge its battery for future needs."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E013","quote":"to ensure that battery charges or discharges neither more than its rated charging and discharging rate nor more than the network limit on the energy transfer."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E014","quote":"E_{transfer}(t) is network controller imposed maximum limit of energy transfer over the network at t such that the node voltage does not violate the recommended limit for reliability and security of the network."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E31","quote":"the total energy charged by the receivers batteries should be equal to the total energy discharged by the providers"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E34","quote":"Please note that in the manuscript we do not consider the scenario, in which a prosumer at state 2 can sell its battery energy to the CPS."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E34","quote":"Nonetheless, if a CPS can participate in the local market as a peer and give prosumers to negotiate the price for per unit energy it would sell to the CPS, the proposed model can easily be extended by including CPS as a peer. In such cases, a prosumer can also sell its energy to the CPS through P2P mechanism."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"1513ffd1e0896b3b65884f2c4ee7eb477202748c0c808d75ca143ae537386069","applicability":"formalized","reason":"C007 and C008 are supported by exact source expressions, and C011 formalizes the e_max definition printed in Eq. (8). The remaining constraints are procedural, scope, price-reference, or boundary statements without a source expression suitable for this frozen rule; the state-2 price formulas in (18)-(19) are intentionally left to the pricing rule scope rather than duplicated here. B evidence remains UNREVIEWED.","bindings":[{"object_id":"x_R003_enc","node_ids":["execution.energy_trade"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Source Eq. (7) bounds e_{n,c}(t) for state-2 battery exchange; the frozen rule assigns this battery-exchange feasibility constraint to execution.energy_trade. No frozen edge carries C007 directly, so no edge is attached."},{"object_id":"x_R003_end","node_ids":["execution.energy_trade"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Source Eq. (7) bounds e_{n,d}(t) for state-2 battery exchange; the frozen rule assigns this battery-exchange feasibility constraint to execution.energy_trade. No frozen edge carries C007 directly, so no edge is attached."},{"object_id":"theta_R003_Echrdis","node_ids":["execution.energy_trade"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"E_{chr,dis} is the battery rating parameter entering the e_{\\max}(t) bound on state-2 battery exchange, supporting the execution.energy_trade feasibility limit."},{"object_id":"theta_R003_Etransfer","node_ids":["execution.energy_trade"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"E_{transfer}(t) is the network-controller transfer limit entering the e_{\\max}(t) bound on state-2 battery exchange, supporting the execution.energy_trade feasibility limit."},{"object_id":"theta_R003_emax","node_ids":["execution.energy_trade"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"e_{\\max}(t) is the effective maximum rate used in the C007 battery-exchange bounds attached to execution.energy_trade; its source definition is formalized in C011."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C007","node_ids":["execution.energy_trade"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C007 is a state-2 battery-exchange feasibility constraint assigned by C.1 to execution.energy_trade. The expression is a faithful transcription of source Eq. (7), preserving the printed s_n form and the e_{\\max}(t) bound."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C008","node_ids":["execution.energy_trade"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C008 is the source's aggregate state-2 battery-energy balance E_d(t)=E_c(t), assigned by C.1 to execution.energy_trade. E_d(t) and E_c(t) are source-defined aggregate totals, not declared as x or theta because they are derived from provider/receiver quantities."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R003-C011","node_ids":["execution.energy_trade"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C011 formalizes the Eq. (8) definition of e_{\\max}(t) used by C007. It is a supporting mathematical constraint on execution.energy_trade; no frozen edge is listed for this added supporting constraint."}],"revision_requests":["Source Eq. (8) prints e_{\\max}(t)=\\max(E_{chr,dis}, E_{transfer}(t)) while the surrounding text says battery charging or discharging must exceed neither the rated rate nor the network transfer limit; B/C.1 should confirm whether the printed max is intentional before operational use of e_{\\max}(t).","C.1 did not expose typed variable or parameter objects. C.2 declares only e_{n,c}, e_{n,d}, E_{chr,dis}, E_{transfer}(t), and e_{\\max}(t) for C007/C011; units and nonnegativity of e_{n,c}/e_{n,d} are not explicitly specified in the supplied pages.","E_d(t) and E_c(t) in C008 are source-defined aggregate totals not declared as x or theta because they are derived from provider/receiver quantities; C.1 may consider a derived-quantity representation if the schema later supports it."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
